Well, Hearse certainly does liven-up the game while
deadening-up the player character; as I just said
and have reason to say again.

"Penny" may not have been the PC name of the final
perpetrator's PC; I found two tombstones labeled
"Penny", one killed by a dog, the other by a Killer
Bee.  Neither tombstone was populate by a ghost or a
bones pile, so somewhere I didn't reach was the
tombstone of the scoundrel who left a silver dragon
in toplevel Mines. My XL4 ranger was nowhere close
to able to cope.

Which raises an interesting issue with Hearse.  It's
all very well to play to keep your PC as low level
as possible, right up _until_ you start importing
bones files from players with a different enough
philosophy that their much higher level PCs result
in bones files populated with high level monsters.

Suddenly, reaching XL30 before DL4 and a possible
bones file is starting to look awfully attractive as
a way to survive some of these monster-laden bones
files.

There are times you are just _so_ happy a game
_doesn't_ drop a bones file to continue the trend.
This was definitely one of them.

